{
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "copy-and-reference",
      "text": "Start with a zero Value and copy it before any method call. A Store on the original leaves the copy empty, so its Load returns nil. If a caller instead saves a pointer to the original and then rebinds its own pointer variable to another Value, the saved pointer still loads from the original. With that saved pointer unchanged, a same-type Store through it replaces what a later Load through it returns. Line 13 prohibits copying once Store has been called, while line 15 prohibits copying after first use. The difference matters if Load, Swap, or CompareAndSwap runs before any Store call.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 15,
        "fragment": "// A Value must not be copied after first use."
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the Store-specific wording be broadened, and does first use include a Load of an empty Value, an unsuccessful CompareAndSwap, or a call that panics before writing?"
    },
    {
      "id": "first-publication",
      "text": "Start with an empty Value. Load returns nil if its type read sees either the empty state or the first-write marker, including after the writer has set the data but before it publishes the concrete type. A Store, Swap, or CompareAndSwap that is eligible to initialize the Value must win the marker CAS. A contender that loses that CAS unpins and retries. Writers that observe the marker spin until publication, then follow their initialized-value branches. The winner writes data, publishes type, unpins, and completes. A Load that reads the published type returns a value of that type and can observe a subsequent same-type replacement.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 31,
        "fragment": "if typ == nil || typ == unsafe.Pointer(&firstStoreInProgress) {"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "store-and-swap-controls",
      "text": "Starting empty with sequential calls, Store(x) makes Load return x. Starting empty again, Swap(x) returns nil and makes Load return x. Once initialized, a same-type Store overwrites the current value, and a same-type Swap overwrites it and returns the value it replaced. Either method panics on a nil interface before attempting a write, or on a different concrete type after observing the initialized type. A typed nil pointer is a non-nil interface and can initialize the Value or replace an existing value of that pointer type.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 124,
        "fragment": "op.typ, op.data = np.typ, SwapPointer(&vp.data, np.data)"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "cas-validation-order",
      "text": "CompareAndSwap first panics if new is a nil interface, then panics if old has a concrete type different from new's, even on an empty Value. After those checks, a call that observes an empty Value returns false for non-nil old, or attempts initialization for nil old and returns true if it wins publication. If it observes the first-write marker, it waits for publication. With an initialized Value, it panics if new's concrete type differs from the stored type even when old is nil. With matching types and nil old, it returns false. Thus a call with nil old and a different-type new can initialize an empty Value if it wins admission, or panic if another concrete type is published first.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 141,
        "fragment": "if op.typ != nil && np.typ != op.typ {"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "cas-equality-limits",
      "text": "With an initialized Value and matching concrete types, CompareAndSwap reconstructs the current interface and compares it with old using Go interface equality. For integers without interference, equal values allow replacement and return true, while unequal values return false without writing. A slice can be stored and loaded, and CompareAndSwap(nil, slice) can initialize an empty Value. A later CompareAndSwap that compares a stored slice with an old slice of the same type panics at equality, before attempting replacement. With nil old and a same-type slice as new, that initialized call returns false.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 183,
        "fragment": "if i != old {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the CompareAndSwap contract explicitly document the equality panic for matching dynamic types whose values cannot be compared?"
    },
    {
      "id": "cas-intervening-write",
      "text": "Start with an initialized Value whose current value equals old. CompareAndSwap loads the data pointer and checks value equality, then makes one pointer CAS. If another same-type write leaves a different data pointer in place when that CAS runs, the call returns false without retrying, even if the intervening value is equal to old. If intervening writes restore the sampled pointer before the final CAS, the call can succeed. In the reverse order, if the CAS succeeds first, a later Store or Swap can replace its result while the CAS still returns true.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 186,
        "fragment": "return CompareAndSwapPointer(&vp.data, data, np.data)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Is the public contract meant to permit false when the value compared equal to old and an intervening write replaced it with an equal value at a different data pointer?"
    }
  ]
}
